      "side": 1,
      "message": "@Gao BTW, here\u0027s a WIP cover letter that covers the motivation in a bit more detail, let me know what you think!:\n\n```\nOverview \u0026 Motivation\n\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\nRead-only EROFS system partitions (such as Android dynamic partitions and\ncontainer rootfs images) typically rely on stacking block-level dm-verity\nbeneath EROFS. While functional, verifying a compressed filesystem at the\n4 KiB block layer introduces several structural inefficiencies:\n1. Cold-cache Merkle leaf I/O amplification \u0026 workqueue fallback:\n   Across a ~3.15 GiB Android image set, a 4 KiB dm-verity Merkle tree\n   occupies ~24.8 MiB (~6,350 blocks). On cold random reads, an uncached\n   Merkle leaf triggers a second dependent random I/O through dm-bufio.\n   Even when dm-verity is configured with `try_verify_in_tasklet`, a\n   dm-bufio cache miss (or a large I/O) returns -EAGAIN in softirq and \n   punts verification to the unbound `kverityd` workqueue, adding \n   scheduling latency.\n2. Verification granularity mismatch with compressed pclusters:\n   EROFS reads and decompresses compressed data in physical clusters\n   (pclusters, e.g. 32 KiB-128 KiB), whereas dm-verity splits, hashes,\n   and verifies every 4 KiB block independently and stores one 32-byte\n   leaf hash per 4 KiB block rather than one hash per pcluster.\n\nDesign \u0026 Security Model\n\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\nThis series introduces native EROFS verification (`EROFS_FEATURE_INCOMPAT_VERITY`,\n`-o root_digest\u003d\u003c64-hex\u003e`), together with 4 standalone EROFS bugfixes and\noptimizations at the bottom of the stack:\n1. Root of trust (`-o root_digest\u003d\u003c64-hex\u003e`):\n   At mount time, `erofs_init_verity()` reads Block 0 and verifies\n   `SHA-256(block0) \u003d\u003d root_digest`. This authenticates `erofs_super_block`\n   and the 80-byte `struct erofs_verity_sb_ext` superblock extension\n   containing `meta_verity_startblk`, `meta_verity_blocks`, `verity_salt`,\n   and `meta_verity_root_digest`. The salted SHA-256 state\n   (`sbi-\u003everity_init_ctx`) is precomputed once at mount time.\n2. Pinned 128-ary Metadata Merkle Tree (Zero Runtime Merkle Disk I/Os):\n   `mkfs.erofs -E verity` places all filesystem metadata (inodes, xattrs,\n   directory blocks, inline files/symlinks, extent tables, and flat digest\n   tables) into a contiguous metadata zone `[meta_verity_startblk,\n   +meta_verity_blocks)`.\n   Because EROFS metadata accounts for only ~0.5% of total image size\n   (~16.8 MiB across all 8 Android dynamic partitions), a 128-ary SHA-256\n   Merkle tree over the metadata zone is only 164 KiB total (41 blocks\n   across all 8 partitions; 60 KiB for `/system`).\n   `erofs_init_verity()` reads and pins the metadata Merkle leaf blocks\n   in RAM at mount time, verifies their L1/root digest against\n   `meta_verity_root_digest`, and `erofs_bread()` verifies each 4 KiB\n   metadata page on first read (caching verified status in\n   `sbi-\u003emeta_verified_bitmap`). Compromised on-disk metadata or digest\n   lists are rejected in `erofs_bread()` with `-EIO` before any inode or\n   extent is parsed.\n3. Co-located Data Digests (Zero Extra Hash-Block I/O on Cold Data Reads):\n   Because EROFS already reads and verifies the inode/extent metadata block\n   via `erofs_bread()` to map a file\u0027s physical block address, storing data\n   digests in that metadata eliminates the second cold random I/O needed to\n   fetch an uncached Merkle leaf block. Only 2 inode layouts carry data\n   digests:\n   - `EROFS_INODE_COMPRESSED_FULL`: Uses the existing `Z_EROFS_ADVISE_EXTRECSZ`\n     variable-size extent record mechanism in `erofs_fs.h` to emit 64-byte\n     `struct z_erofs_extent_verity` records (32-byte `z_erofs_extent` +\n     32-byte salted SHA-256 digest covering the entire compressed pcluster).\n   - `EROFS_INODE_FLAT_PLAIN`: Stores a contiguous `nblocks * 32B` salted\n     SHA-256 digest table immediately following the inode/xattrs for\n     uncompressed files \u003e\u003d 4 KiB. Contiguous readahead pages are batched\n     into 64-page bios and verified in pairs via 2-way SIMD\n     `sha256_finup_2x()`.\n   - Small (\u003c 4 KiB) files, directories, and symlinks use\n     `EROFS_INODE_FLAT_INLINE` inside the metadata zone and are covered\n     directly by the metadata Merkle tree with zero extra digest structures.\n4. SoftIRQ \u0026 Multi-CPU Verification/Decompression Sharding:\n   For compressed reads, the head pcluster is claimed in `bi_end_io`\n   softirq context and verified concurrently while the reader thread is\n   waking up. Multi-pcluster readahead chains are sharded across per-CPU\n   `[erofs_worker]` kthreads so SHA-256 verification and LZ4 decompression\n   scale across online CPUs.",
